---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { SITE_NAME } from '../../config/site';

const path = '/about/how-we-create-content';
const title = 'How FixUpWise Creates Its Guides and Calculators';
const description =
  'How FixUpWise selects topics, builds calculator formulas, cites sources, labels assumptions, discloses AI illustrations, and updates published pages.';
---

<PageLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path={path}
  breadcrumbs={[
    { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
    { label: 'About', href: '/about' },
    { label: 'How We Create Content' },
  ]}
>
  <p slot="lede" class="section-lede">
    An honest look at how {SITE_NAME} builds practical homeowner guides and quantity calculators —
    and what we do not claim.
  </p>

  <h2>How topics are selected</h2>
  <p>
    Topics start from common planning questions homeowners ask before buying materials: how much volume,
    how many bags or gallons, how coverage rates work, and which assumptions change the result. Priority
    goes to questions where transparent math and labeled defaults help more than marketing copy.
  </p>

  <h2>How calculator formulas are built</h2>
  <p>
    Each calculator applies a documented formula to the inputs you enter (area, depth, coats, tile size,
    and similar). Defaults such as coverage rates, bag yields, and waste factors are labeled and editable
    where the tool allows. The math is deterministic for those inputs — the same numbers produce the same
    result.
  </p>

  <h2>Sources and product data</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Manufacturer documentation and technical data sheets when a product yield, bag volume, or coverage
      rate is used.
    </li>
    <li>
      University extension and other authoritative public guidance where it adds planning context (for
      example mulch depth ranges).
    </li>
    <li>
      One manufacturer’s specification is never treated as universal for every similar product — check the
      label on what you buy.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Calculations vs. recommendations</h2>
  <p>
    Deterministic math answers quantity questions for a given size and assumption set. Recommendations —
    how thick a slab should be, how deep to mulch, whether two coats are enough — are planning context,
    not engineering or code approval. Articles label which statements are examples, defaults, or ranges.
  </p>

  <h2>Assumptions and sources on the page</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Assumptions such as bag yields, coverage rates, waste factors, and optional extras are labeled.</li>
    <li>Cited sources appear in article source lists when used.</li>
    <li>
      Articles and calculators are updated when source guidance or calculator logic changes in a way that
      affects published numbers or framing.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Illustrations</h2>
  <p>
    Some diagrams and illustrations on {SITE_NAME} are AI-generated for explanatory use and are labeled
    accordingly (typically: “Illustration: AI-generated for explanatory use.”). Ordinary photography is
    not given that caption. {SITE_NAME} does not present AI-generated people or project photos as real
    completed work.
  </p>

  <h2>What we do not do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Fabricate project results or field tests we have not performed.</li>
    <li>Invent expert quotes, biographies, or contractor credentials.</li>
    <li>Claim licensed contractor review, engineering approval, or laboratory testing unless that review actually happened.</li>
    <li>Replace local code requirements or manufacturer instructions with generic advice.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Corrections and updates</h2>
  <p>
    Factual, mathematical, or sourcing errors may be corrected when found, and affected pages updated.
    {SITE_NAME} does not operate a formal newsroom corrections desk. Meaningful changes to calculator
    logic or cited figures are reflected on the relevant pages; when public article dates are shown,
    updated dates will follow the site’s publication-date policy.
  </p>

  <h2>Planning estimates, not professional design</h2>
  <p>
    Calculators and worked examples help you estimate quantities before you buy. They are not
    structural engineering, building-code approval, architectural plans, or contractor bids. Confirm
    measurements on site, read the label on the product you purchase, and get qualified help when a
    project involves structural, electrical, plumbing, or other safety-critical decisions.
  </p>

  <p>
    Related: <a href="/about">About {SITE_NAME}</a> · <a href="/disclaimer">Disclaimer</a> ·
    <a href="/contact">Contact</a>
  </p>
</PageLayout>
